<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Taillebourg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">21–22 July <a href="/years/1242">1242</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge-war">Saintonge War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Taillebourg was fought on 21–22 July 1242 at the bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a> at Taillebourg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a> and his allies the Lusignans, and a further victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintes">Saintes</a> followed. The failure ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s attempt to recover Poitou and doomed the revolt of Raymond VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-taillebourg.jpg" alt="Battle of Taillebourg" width="320" height="148" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chroniques de Saint-Denis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis9_vs_Henri3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By his will Louis VIII had left the title of Count of Poitou to his younger son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-count-of-poitiers">Alphonse of Poitiers</a>. In June 1241 Louis IX held a plenary court at Saumur and announced that Alphonse, now of age, would take possession, then installed him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>. The Lusignans opposed Capetian authority in the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-angouleme">Isabella of Angoulême</a> resented that her son Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a>, had not received the title. Her husband, Hugh X of Lusignan, Count of La Marche, raised an army, and talks failed.</p><p>In April 1242 Louis gathered a force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinon">Chinon</a>, put at around 50,000 by some contemporaries and at 25,000 by modern historians, and took many rebel castles. Henry III landed at Royan on 20 May and joined the rebels, forming an army that modern estimates place at around 30,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 21 July the armies faced each other across the bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a>, a strategic crossing on the route between northern and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Louis and Alphonse held the Château de Taillebourg above it. The English and the rebels attacked to take the bridge, but a massive charge by French knights sallying from the castle drove them back toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintes">Saintes</a>. Henry and Hugh fled separately, leaving their army without leaders, and on 22 July a prolonged melee north of Saintes ended in a second defeat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After a short siege the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintes">Saintes</a> handed Louis the keys of the city. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> by sea failed, and in January 1243 Henry told Emperor Frederick II that he had given up hope of retaking his French possessions. A five-year truce was signed at Pons on 1 August, and a lasting peace came at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 4 December 1259, under which Louis restored Guyenne to Henry. Hugh's Poitevin castles were confiscated. The battle ended Henry's hopes of restoring the Angevin Empire, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugene-delacroix">Eugène Delacroix</a> painted it for the Salon of 1837.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Taillebourg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The French crown conquered Poitou between 1204 and 1224, when Louis VIII took La Rochelle, and Henry III's attempt to recover it failed at the <b>Battle of Taillebourg</b> in 1242.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Military</a></li></ul></section>
